At nineteen, after a short spell playing professional football for Dundee FC, I moved to the United States on a soccer scholarship at Radford University where I met my wife Fallon during our freshman year. She's from Dandridge, Tennessee, located close to the foothills of the Great Smoky Mountains. We spent our summers and holidays throughout our college years on Douglas Lake or exploring the mountains. Having grown up between the coast and mountains, I fell in love with East Tennessee immediately.

Photography started as a way of sharing those experiences with family back home. My mum had a Pentax K30, and every time I visited Scotland or my parents came to visit me, I'd take custody of it and shoot as much as I could. When I graduated from Radford, I used most of the money I had to buy a Canon Rebel T1i and a couple of lenses. That's where the passion really took hold. Every trip we took and every place we visited, I was shooting and experimenting and figuring out what I was looking for.

Over the next several years I built a professional creative career in photography and eventually found my way into production, first shooting branded content and weddings, then taking on more of the operational side of running shoots. Now I'm based in Knoxville permanently, where I run production operations at Big Slate Media, a video production company with around a dozen creatives working across twenty-plus projects at any given time.

Photography remains the throughline. My family fills most of these pages; my wife, our kids, the adventures we go on. Occasionally I get to do it for someone else, and that's a privilege I don't take lightly.
